{"authored" [{"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/review.json" "file_sha256" "408c688fdeb7565fcbca5bc600f357358c7a1c6be179094cc1554b7f5d361059" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "881f5ffd2b4749dab93362d969c575b17002c692170702458d6ae9226b1b4552" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/verification.json" "basis" 1739 "rationale" "The actual example integrands are continuous, but the universal symmetry rule must require existence of the ordinary integral. Qualify the rule and explicitly distinguish ordinary improper convergence from principal-value regularization. Original source prompt, answer fields, and historical original solution remain preserved."} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/review.json" "file_sha256" "408c688fdeb7565fcbca5bc600f357358c7a1c6be179094cc1554b7f5d361059" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "881f5ffd2b4749dab93362d969c575b17002c692170702458d6ae9226b1b4552" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/verification.json" "basis" 1739 "rationale" "The actual example integrands are continuous, but the universal symmetry rule must require existence of the ordinary integral. Qualify the rule and explicitly distinguish ordinary improper convergence from principal-value regularization. Original source prompt, answer fields, and historical original solution remain preserved."} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/example-10911.json" "file_sha256" "0d08b282b42d4238e9813bbf0fb3765d4f1f75bbfe069eaf2b06fe5c3dc72243" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/example-10911.json" "file_sha256" "0d08b282b42d4238e9813bbf0fb3765d4f1f75bbfe069eaf2b06fe5c3dc72243" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/r